  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>West Carrollton is a small city with easy access to everything the Dayton area offers. It sits in Montgomery County just south of Dayton, along the Great Miami River, with quick access to I-75 and SR-725. Whether you are relocating for work, moving closer to family, or just looking for a quieter place to live, a little planning makes the transition a lot smoother.</p>
<p>Here are twelve things to handle before and after your move, in roughly the order you will need them. For a printable, step-by-step version, use our <a href="/tools/moving-checklist/">moving checklist tool</a>.</p>

<h2>Before You Move</h2>
<h3>1. Lock in your apartment and read the lease</h3>
<p>Once you pick a place, read the lease carefully before signing. Note the move-in date, what utilities you are responsible for, pet rules, parking, and any move-in fees. Take photos and notes of the unit's condition on move-in day so you have a record for your security deposit. If you are still searching, browse <a href="/apartments/">West Carrollton apartments</a> by size and budget.</p>
<h3>2. Estimate your moving costs</h3>
<p>Truck rental, movers, packing supplies, deposits, and first month's rent add up fast. Run the numbers early with our <a href="/tools/moving-cost-estimator/">moving cost estimator</a> so there are no surprises.</p>
<h3>3. Set up utilities</h3>
<p>Ask your property manager which utilities are included and which you need to set up in your own name. Common ones include electricity, natural gas, water and sewer, trash, and internet. Call or go online with each provider at least a week or two before move-in so service is active when you arrive. Internet installation in particular can take a while to schedule, so book it early if you work from home.</p>
<h3>4. Forward your mail</h3>
<p>Submit a change of address with the U.S. Postal Service a couple of weeks before your move. You can do it online or at a post office. Mail forwarding is a safety net, not a permanent fix, so you will still want to update your address directly with important accounts.</p>
<h3>5. Update your address everywhere else</h3>
<p>Make a list and work through it: your bank and credit cards, employer and payroll, insurance providers, doctors and pharmacy, subscriptions, and any online shopping accounts. Do not forget your renters insurance policy, which should list your new address before you move in.</p>
<h3>6. Pack with the new space in mind</h3>
<p>Measure your new apartment, or ask for a floor plan, and decide where big furniture will go before moving day. Our <a href="/tools/room-planner/">room planner</a> helps you test layouts so you do not haul a couch that will not fit.</p>

<!--products-->

<h2>After You Arrive</h2>
<h3>7. Get your Ohio driver license</h3>
<p>If you are moving from another state, Ohio generally expects new residents to get an Ohio driver license within 30 days of establishing residency. Visit an Ohio BMV deputy registrar location and bring proof of identity, your Social Security number, and proof of Ohio residency such as your lease or a utility bill. Requirements can change, so check the Ohio BMV website for the current document list before you go.</p>
<h3>8. Title and register your vehicle</h3>
<p>Out-of-state vehicles also need to be titled and registered in Ohio, generally within the same 30-day window. Out-of-state vehicles typically need an inspection, which some deputy registrar offices and dealerships can perform. Again, confirm current steps with the BMV before making the trip.</p>
<h3>9. Register to vote</h3>
<p>Once you have a new address, register to vote or update your registration. Ohio offers online registration through the Secretary of State, and you can also register by mail or in person with the Montgomery County Board of Elections. To vote in a given election, you need to be registered at least 30 days beforehand.</p>
<h3>10. Get a Dayton Metro Library card</h3>
<p>West Carrollton is part of the Dayton Metro Library system, which has a branch in the city. A library card gets you books, movies, and access to the system's digital collections and online resources. Libraries are also a great place to find local event listings and community programs when you are new in town.</p>
<h3>11. Explore the parks and the Great Miami River Trail</h3>
<p>One of the best parts of living in West Carrollton is being close to the Great Miami River Trail, a long paved multi-use trail that follows the river through the Miami Valley. It is great for walking, running, and biking, and it connects to a much larger regional trail network. The city also has several neighborhood parks to explore. Spend a weekend or two getting outside and learning the area.</p>
<h3>12. Meet your neighbors and learn the area</h3>
<p>Find your nearest grocery store, pharmacy, gas station, and urgent care before you need them. Introduce yourself to a few neighbors, too. A friendly hello early on makes the building feel like home faster. Our guide to <a href="/blog/getting-to-know-your-neighbors/">getting to know your neighbors</a> has easy, low-pressure ideas.</p>

<h2>Bonus Tips for Your First Month</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Test your smoke detectors</strong> and find the water shutoff and breaker box in your unit.</li>
<li><strong>Save your property manager's contact info</strong>, including the after-hours maintenance number.</li>
<li><strong>Learn trash and recycling days</strong> for your building.</li>
<li><strong>Plan your commute</strong> at the times you will actually drive, especially if you will be on I-75 toward Dayton during rush hour.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare for winter</strong> if you are coming from a warmer climate. An ice scraper, a warm coat, and a small emergency kit for your car go a long way in Ohio.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Settling In</h2>
<p>Moving is a lot of small tasks, and it is easy to forget one in the chaos. Tackle the paperwork items, like your license, registration, and voter registration, in your first few weeks so they do not linger. Then give yourself time to enjoy the new place. For a deeper look at neighborhoods, things to do, and daily life here, read our <a href="/west-carrollton-living-guide/">West Carrollton living guide</a>.</p>
